WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:04.440
Is this too much?

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.720
Gallup has been tracking American

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.300
attitudes on global warming for more
than two decades. In 1997, 48 percent

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.800
nationally said that the effects of global
warming had already begun. In Gallup's new

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.869
March poll, 60 percent gave that response.
Just 12 percent said that the effects

00:00:21.869 --> 00:00:27.180
would never happen. However, a majority in
Gallup's new poll did not believe global

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:31.260
warming would pose a serious threat to
them during their lifetimes. That may be

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.370
one reason why the issue ranks low when
pollsters asked about top priorities for

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:40.440
the President and Congress. In a January
Pew survey, 46 percent said that global

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:46.110
climate change should be a top priority.
Of the 19 issues Pew asked about, climate

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:51.870
change tied for second to last place.
More people – 62 percent – said that protecting the

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:56.399
environment should be a top priority.
Terrorism outstripped both as a top

00:00:56.399 --> 00:01:00.960
priority at 73 percent. In Gallup's polling,
the public worries more about

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.589
environmental problems they can see in
their own communities, such as the

00:01:04.589 --> 00:01:07.820
pollution of drinking water, than they
worry about climate change.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:12.200
Are you concerned about climate change? 
Why or why not?

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.600
Let us know in your comments. Also, let us
know what other topics you'd like our

00:01:16.619 --> 00:01:21.060
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be
sure to like and subscribe to more

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.470
research and videos from AEI.

